---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CaseStudy from '../../components/CaseStudy.astro';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Work', url: '/work/' },
  { name: 'AI Agent Chatbot', url: '/work/ai-agent-chatbot/' },
];

const technologies = [
  'Python',
  'Claude API',
  'Text-to-SQL',
  'SQLite',
  'Natural Language Processing',
  'Prompt Engineering',
];
---
<Base
  title="AI Agent Chatbot for Energy Data"
  description="A trustworthy natural language question-answering system over U.S. electricity generation data with checked query translation and refusal mechanisms."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  articleDate="2024-01-15"
>
  <main class="content-page">
    <CaseStudy
      title="AI Agent Chatbot for Energy Data"
      category="Trustworthy AI · Natural Language"
      description="A natural language question-answering system that demonstrates how to build trustworthy AI for data queries—with safety checks, parameter binding, and refusal when questions can't be answered."
      githubUrl="https://github.com/likarajo/ai-agent-chatbot"
      technologies={technologies}
    >
      <section>
        <h2>Overview</h2>
        <p>
          This project demonstrates a trustworthy approach to natural language question-answering
          over structured data. The system converts plain-language questions about U.S. electricity
          generation into safe, parameterized SQL queries while refusing questions that cannot be
          answered from the available data.
        </p>
        <p>
          The chatbot uses public data from the U.S. Energy Information Administration (EIA),
          covering over 50 years of electricity generation broken down by state and energy source
          (coal, natural gas, nuclear, wind, solar, etc.).
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The Challenge</h2>
        <p>
          Natural language interfaces to databases face several critical challenges:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>SQL Injection Risk:</strong> Naively inserting user text into SQL queries
            creates severe security vulnerabilities
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Schema Misalignment:</strong> Users may ask questions the database cannot answer,
            leading to incorrect results or hallucinated data
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Access Control:</strong> Query systems must respect data permissions and not
            leak restricted information
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Accuracy Verification:</strong> Generated queries must be checked before
            execution to ensure they match the question's intent
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Solution Approach</h2>

        <h3>1. Checked Query Translation</h3>
        <p>
          Instead of directly converting questions to SQL, the system uses a two-step process:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            Extract the semantic intent: What aggregations, filters, and dimensions does the
            question require?
          </li>
          <li>
            Map that intent to a predefined query template with parameters—never insert user
            text directly into SQL
          </li>
        </ol>

        <div class="highlight-box">
          <h3>Example: Wind Generation in Texas</h3>
          <p><strong>Question:</strong> "How much wind generation did Texas produce in 2023?"</p>
          <p><strong>Intent Extracted:</strong></p>
          <ul>
            <li>Year: 2023</li>
            <li>State: Texas</li>
            <li>Energy Source: Wind</li>
            <li>Metric: Total generation (megawatthours)</li>
          </ul>
          <p><strong>Query:</strong> A parameterized SELECT with these values bound safely</p>
          <p><strong>Result:</strong> 119,855,132 megawatthours (verified against EIA data)</p>
        </div>

        <h3>2. Refusal Mechanism</h3>
        <p>
          When a question asks for data that doesn't exist in the schema, the system refuses
          and explains why:
        </p>
        <div class="highlight-box">
          <p><strong>Question:</strong> "What was customer revenue in Texas in 2023?"</p>
          <p><strong>Response:</strong> The system explains that the electricity generation table
          has no revenue column and cannot answer this question. No query is executed.</p>
        </div>

        <h3>3. Schema Awareness</h3>
        <p>
          The agent has access to the complete database schema, allowing it to:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Validate that requested columns and tables exist</li>
          <li>Check that filters and aggregations are appropriate for the data types</li>
          <li>Identify when questions require joins or calculations the schema doesn't support</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>4. Security by Design</h3>
        <p>Key security principles implemented:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Parameter Binding:</strong> All user inputs are bound as query parameters,
            never concatenated into SQL strings
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Query Validation:</strong> Every generated query is checked against allowed
            patterns before execution
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Read-Only Access:</strong> The system operates with read-only database
            permissions
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No Direct SQL:</strong> Users cannot provide raw SQL—only natural language
            questions
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Technical Implementation</h2>

        <h3>Architecture</h3>
        <p>The system consists of several components:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Language Model (Claude):</strong> Interprets questions and maps them to
            database operations
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Query Builder:</strong> Constructs parameterized SQL from semantic intent
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Schema Validator:</strong> Checks that requested operations are valid for
            the schema
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Execution Engine:</strong> Runs validated queries with bound parameters
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Data Source</h3>
        <p>
          The chatbot uses the EIA's Electric Power Annual dataset, which provides:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Historical electricity generation data from 1970–present</li>
          <li>Breakdown by U.S. state and energy source</li>
          <li>Generation measured in megawatthours</li>
          <li>Public domain data suitable for demonstration purposes</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Key Takeaways</h2>

        <div class="highlight-box">
          <h3>Principles for Trustworthy Text-to-SQL</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <strong>Never inject user text into SQL:</strong> Use parameterized queries
              exclusively
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Make refusal explicit:</strong> When the system can't answer, it should
              say why, not guess
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Validate before execution:</strong> Check that generated queries match
              the question's intent
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Expose schema constraints:</strong> The AI needs to know what data exists
              and what doesn't
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Design for auditability:</strong> Query translation steps should be
              inspectable and logged
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Use Cases</h2>
        <p>
          This approach is applicable to domains where data accuracy and security are critical:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Energy infrastructure and grid operations data</li>
          <li>Financial reporting and compliance queries</li>
          <li>Healthcare data analysis with privacy requirements</li>
          <li>Government and public sector data transparency</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Future Directions</h2>
        <p>Potential extensions to explore:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Multi-table queries with join safety checks</li>
          <li>Time-series analysis and forecasting questions</li>
          <li>Row-level access control based on user permissions</li>
          <li>Query result explanation and provenance tracking</li>
          <li>Integration with data governance frameworks</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <div style="margin-top: 3rem; padding-top: 2rem; border-top: 2px solid #e0e0e0;">
        <p>
          <a href="/work/">← Back to all projects</a> |
          <a href="https://github.com/likarajo/ai-agent-chatbot" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">View on GitHub →</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </CaseStudy>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .content-page {
    max-width: 800px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 2rem 1rem;
  }

  section {
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  a {
    color: #4a90e2;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
</style>
